% ============================================================================
% syllable-substitution — changing one syllable in a word to form a new word,
% a SEVENTH literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Deliberately DIFFERENT in shape from all six prior literacy libraries:
% `word-families.adj` derives RHYMING, `syllable-count.adj` recalls a
% SYLLABLE COUNT, `onset-rime.adj` recalls an ONSET/RIME split,
% `initial-sound.adj` recalls a BEGINNING sound, `phoneme-substitution.adj`
% recalls the new word formed by swapping one SOUND, and
% `phoneme-deletion.adj` recalls the new word formed by removing one sound.
% This library recalls what happens when you substitute a whole SYLLABLE (not
% a single phoneme) in a multi-syllable word, grounding a syllable-level
% analogue of the phonemic-awareness continuum as a pure lookup:
% `syllable_substitution(original_word, new_word, changed_position)`. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? syllable_substitution(suntan, sunset, $Position)   % second
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table like
% `onset-rime.adj`): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `syllable_substitution(original_word, new_word, changed_position)` carrying
% the Reading Rockets citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the changed position WITH its source, on
% the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any word pair not the one
% the cited page walks through. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a
% position and recall the word pair that changes there:
%
%     ? syllable_substitution($Orig, $New, second)   % suntan, sunset
%
% The one substitution this table carries, as a little truth table:
%
%     original_word   new_word   changed_position
%     -------------   --------   ----------------
%     suntan          sunset     second
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module — the SAME already-vetted page
% `syllable-count.adj`, `phoneme-substitution.adj`, and `phoneme-deletion.adj`
% already cite (its "Substituting Syllables" section), WebFetch-verified
% TWICE for consistency before writing. The exact spans, composed together
% the same way `phoneme-substitution.adj`'s and `phoneme-deletion.adj`'s own
% rows already are (two distinct CLEAN PROSE sentences from the same cited
% section, each stating one fact — deliberately NOT drawing from the
% section's bracketed stage-direction text, which names the literal old/new
% syllable text ("tan"/"set") but is instructional stage direction rather
% than a stated fact):
%
%   "I will change 'suntan' to 'sunset'."
%     (states the transformation: suntan -> sunset)
%   "The second syllable is different."
%     (states which syllable position changed: second)
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier `phoneme-substitution.adj`'s,
% `phoneme-deletion.adj`'s, and `syllable-count.adj`'s sibling sections of
% this page already earned. Deliberately scoped to ONLY this ONE
% substitution the cited page walks through step by step, and to the
% POSITION fact its clean prose sentences state (not the literal syllable
% text, which only appears inside bracketed stage directions) — honestly
% narrow (mirroring `phoneme-deletion.adj`'s and `onset-rime.adj`'s
% precedent) rather than inventing a syllable-text value the source's own
% prose does not state. A future slice can add a second substitution, or a
% syllable-text column, once a comparably clean citation is found. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table syllable_substitution {
    columns original_word, new_word, changed_position

    row (suntan, sunset, second)

    source "I will change 'suntan' to 'sunset'."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
